  <p>
    Neither path is automatically the "smart" one for everyone. Someone with strong quantitative skills,
    a finance or engineering background, and access to the hiring process for prop firms, banks, or funds
    is usually better served aiming at the professional route first, and testing personal trading later, with
    money they can afford to lose, if at all. Someone drawn purely to the independence of retail trading
    needs to treat the capital and runway math in the next section as non-negotiable, not optional.
  </p>

  <h2 id="capital-and-runway">Capital, runway, and the math nobody shows you</h2>

  <p>
    Almost no "how to start trading" content online talks about the money math honestly. This is the part
    that actually decides whether someone survives long enough to find out if they have a real edge.
  </p>

